        title: "The counseling profession: history, credentials and who regulates what",
        minutes: 9,
        content: "# The counseling profession: history, credentials and who regulates what\n\nThe exam expects you to know how the profession is organized: the difference between a license and a certification, what NBCC does and does not do, and the dates and names that mark the profession's development. This is memorization, and it is finite.\n\n## License versus certification\n\nA license is granted by a state government and is required to practice. It is a legal credential; practicing without it is illegal. Licensure titles vary by state: LPC, LPCC, LMHC, LCPC and others describe the same general role.\n\nA certification is granted by a professional organization and is voluntary. The National Certified Counselor (NCC) credential from NBCC is the most common. It demonstrates that you met a national standard; it does not by itself permit you to practice anywhere. The exam likes to test this distinction directly: the state board licenses, NBCC certifies.\n\n## NBCC, the NCE and the NCMHCE\n\nThe National Board for Certified Counselors was established in 1982 to create a national credential. It owns and administers the National Counselor Examination (NCE) and the National Clinical Mental Health Counseling Examination (NCMHCE). Most state boards use one or both as their licensing examination, which is why you are taking it.\n\nThe NCE has 200 multiple-choice items, 160 scored and 40 unscored pretest items, in 3 hours 45 minutes. It is scored on six domains (Professional Practice and Ethics, Intake, Assessment and Diagnosis, Areas of Clinical Focus, Treatment Planning, Counseling Skills and Interventions, Core Counseling Attributes) whose weights come from a national job analysis, and its items are mapped back to the eight CACREP areas. The NCMHCE uses clinical case simulations rather than stand-alone items.\n\n## Professional associations\n\nThe American Counseling Association (ACA) is the profession's primary association and publishes the Code of Ethics the exam tests. It began in 1952 as the American Personnel and Guidance Association (APGA), became the American Association for Counseling and Development (AACD) in 1983, and the ACA in 1992. Its divisions cover specialties: ASCA for school counselors, AMHCA for mental health counselors, ACES for counselor educators and supervisors, and others.\n\nCACREP, the Council for Accreditation of Counseling and Related Educational Programs, accredits graduate programs. Accreditation attaches to the program, not the person. Chi Sigma Iota is the international honor society for counseling.\n\n## The dates you need\n\nFrank Parsons opened the Vocation Bureau in Boston in 1908 and is called the father of guidance; his book Choosing a Vocation appeared in 1909. Clifford Beers published A Mind That Found Itself in 1908, starting the mental hygiene movement. Carl Rogers published Counseling and Psychotherapy in 1942, shifting the field toward the relationship. The National Defense Education Act of 1958 funded school counselor training after Sputnik. The Community Mental Health Centers Act of 1963 moved treatment out of hospitals. Virginia became the first state to license counselors in 1976. California, in 2009, became the last.\n\n## Portability and the Counseling Compact\n\nBecause licensure is state-based, moving has historically meant reapplying. The Counseling Compact, active since 2022 with member states joining over time, allows a counselor licensed in one member state to obtain a privilege to practice in others. The exam may describe portability generally; know that the compact exists, that it requires a home-state license, and that it is not the same as reciprocity.\n\n## Terms to know\n\n- **Licensure**: State-granted legal permission to practice; mandatory.\n- **Certification**: Voluntary credential from a professional body (NCC from NBCC).\n- **CACREP**: Accredits counselor education programs, not individuals.\n- **Parsons, 1908**: Vocation Bureau; father of the guidance movement.\n- **APGA → AACD → ACA**: 1952, 1983, 1992: the association's three names.\n- **Virginia, 1976**: First state to license counselors.\n\n## On the exam\n\nWhen an item asks who grants a license, the answer is the state board, never NBCC, ACA or CACREP. When it asks who accredits, the answer is CACREP, and it accredits programs.",
