        minutes: 8,
        content: "# Informed consent and professional disclosure\n\nInformed consent is the client's right to know what they are agreeing to. The exam tests its elements, its timing, and what happens when a client cannot give it.\n\n## What informed consent must include\n\nThe Code requires counselors to review, in writing and verbally, the purposes, goals, techniques, procedures, limitations, potential risks and benefits of services; the counselor's qualifications, credentials and relevant experience; continuation of services if the counselor becomes incapacitated; the implications of diagnosis; the intended use of tests and reports; fees and billing; confidentiality and its limits; the client's right to obtain records, participate in planning and refuse services; and the consequences of refusal.\n\nTwo words in the Code matter: consent is an ongoing process, not a one-time signature, and it must be delivered in developmentally and culturally appropriate ways.\n\n## The professional disclosure statement\n\nA professional disclosure statement is the written document that carries most of the informed consent content: credentials, theoretical orientation, fees, confidentiality limits, emergency contact, and how to file a complaint with the licensing board. Many states require it by law. On the exam, \"the document a counselor provides describing credentials and practice\" is the professional disclosure statement.\n\n## Capacity and assent\n\nWhen a client is a minor or lacks capacity to consent, the counselor obtains consent from a parent or legal guardian and seeks the client's assent, meaning their agreement to participate. Assent is not legally binding but is ethically required. The counselor also includes the client in decisions to the extent they are able.\n\n## Consent in special situations\n\nRecording sessions or observation by supervisors requires the client's permission in advance. Distance counseling requires additional consent covering technology risks, emergency procedures across jurisdictions, and the counselor's licensure in the client's location. Research participation requires consent that makes clear participation is voluntary and refusal carries no penalty. Mandated clients, such as court-referred, still receive informed consent, including what will be reported to the referring agency and the consequences of refusing services.\n\n## Terms to know\n\n- **Informed consent**: Ongoing process of ensuring the client understands and agrees to services.\n- **Professional disclosure statement**: Written description of credentials, fees, confidentiality, complaint process.\n- **Assent**: Agreement from a client who cannot legally consent.\n- **Mandated client**: Referred by court or agency; still entitled to informed consent.\n\n## On the exam\n\n\"When should informed consent be obtained?\" The answer is at the beginning of the relationship and throughout, not at the first session only, and not after a treatment plan is set.",
